```elixir
fig_protocol = Vl.new(width: 200, height: 220)
  |> Vl.data_from_values(Enum.filter(tbl_protocols, &(&1.mode == :actor)))
  |> Vl.mark(:bar)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "fixture", type: :nominal, title: "Constructed fixture")
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "accuracy", type: :quantitative, scale: %{domain: [0, 1]})
  |> Vl.encode_field(:column, "protocol", type: :nominal)

Kino.VegaLite.new(fig_protocol)
```

The clock obtains all four signs on the fixed sequence, two with a fresh reset
per case, and zero in its worst permutation. Only four of the 24 permutations
give it all four correct signs. The tanh control classifies every permutation
correctly. These outcomes are asserted for actor, Nx, and hybrid execution.

## 9.3 Retesting Our Neuroevolutionary System

Sher first repeats mutation and execution testing, then varies activation choices
and local-tuning patience in feedforward XOR runs. The following are **six
individual historical observations**, not averages or remaster measurements.
[S, pp. 388-395]

| Allowed activations | Source MAX_ATTEMPTS | Evaluations | Reported result |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| tanh | 50 | 14,806 | Solved |
| tanh | 10 | 5,396 | Solved |
| tanh | 1 | 2,281 | Failed within 100 generations; best fitness about 7 |
| tanh, cos, gaussian, absolute | 50 | 910 | Solved with one bias-free cosine neuron |
| Same mixed set | 10 | 694 | Solved with one bias-free cosine neuron |
| Same mixed set | 1 | 565 | Solved with one bias-free cosine neuron |

### Representation changes the problem

The source's cosine solutions suggest a useful analytical control:

$$
\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}(x_1+x_2)\right)=-x_1x_2,
\qquad x_1,x_2\in\{-1,+1\}.
$$

One bias-free cosine neuron can therefore represent bipolar XOR exactly.
This identity is a derived oracle, not a result found by our evolutionary
search. It does not establish that search will discover those weights quickly.
Likewise, a minimum neuron count for a restricted tanh architecture is not a
universal bound for every possible network design.

```elixir
g_cosine = Chapter09.DiagnosticFixtures.cosine()
xs_xor = Chapter09.Functions.cases()
ys_cosine = Chapter09.Protocol.predict(g_cosine, xs_xor, :tensor)
tbl_cosine = Enum.zip(xs_xor, ys_cosine) |> Enum.map(fn {[x1, x2], predicted} ->
  %{x1: x1, x2: x2, target: -x1 * x2, predicted: predicted}
end)
Chapter09.Tables.tabs(tbl_cosine, [:x1, :x2, :target, :predicted], "ch_09_tbl_06_cosine_oracle.csv")
```

### Retest the implementation before interpreting search

The inherited numerical and neural checks include a known XOR graph, recurrent
impulse response, reset, duplicate signals, and worker cleanup. New checks add
signed-log boundary inputs, duplicate ticks, and exact checkpoint replay at a
generation boundary. Checkpoint replay here is in memory; it does not test Mnesia
durability or every possible cancellation race.

```elixir
tbl_runtime_checks = Chapter09.Checks.core() ++ Chapter09.Checks.neural() ++
  Chapter09.Experiments.numerical_checks() ++ Chapter09.Experiments.checkpoint_checks() ++
  Chapter09.Experiments.duplicate_tick_checks()

Chapter09.Tables.tabs(tbl_runtime_checks, [:check, :result], "ch_09_tbl_07_runtime_checks.csv")
```

### A small, matched search experiment

We use three seeds, two activation families, and patience values 1 and 3.
Each setting runs through all three implementations with the same initial
genotypes and recorded draws.

| Setting | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Population / storage | Four agents / ten slots each |
| Architecture | Feedforward; one primary XOR sensor and actuator |
| Activation families | tanh; tanh + cos + gaussian + absolute |
| Local proposals | At most four per evaluated agent |
| Patience | Stop after one or three consecutive rejected proposals |
| Run limits | Eight generations; 128 fitness evaluations |
| Fitness goal | 10 |
| Structural mutation retries | At most twelve attempts per offspring |
| Seeds | 909, 910, 911 |

One evaluation means one complete four-case fitness episode, including the
baseline before local tuning. Archive verification after a run is a validation
check outside the search budget. The full-generation reservation rule can leave
unused evaluations. Patience 1 still permits local proposals; it is not equivalent
to turning local search off.

The sample is deliberately small. Keep every failed run. A median over successful
runs is undefined when none succeeds, so the table displays `none` rather than
silently dropping failures or assigning them a fake success time.

```elixir
experiment = Chapter09.Experiments.batch([909, 910, 911], [1, 3])
tbl_runs = experiment.rows
Chapter09.Tables.tabs(tbl_runs,
  [:family, :patience, :seed, :mode, :generations, :evaluations, :fitness, :goal_met, :accuracy, :neurons, :fallbacks],
  "ch_09_tbl_08_search_runs.csv")
```

```elixir
tbl_search_summary = experiment.summary
Chapter09.Tables.tabs(tbl_search_summary,
  [:family, :patience, :mode, :runs, :solved, :success_rate, :median_terminal_evaluations,
   :median_success_evaluations, :median_best_fitness],
  "ch_09_tbl_09_search_summary.csv")
```

```elixir
tbl_agreement = experiment.agreement
Chapter09.Tables.tabs(tbl_agreement,
  [:family, :patience, :seed, :counters_match, :scores_within_tolerance],
  "ch_09_tbl_10_implementation_agreement.csv")
```

```elixir
tbl_trace = Enum.filter(experiment.traces, &(&1.mode == :actor))
  |> Enum.map(fn row ->
    setting = "#{row.family}; patience #{row.patience}"
    Map.merge(row, %{setting: setting, series: "#{setting}; seed #{row.seed}"})
  end)

fig_search = Vl.new(width: 660, height: 280)
  |> Vl.data_from_values(tbl_trace)
  |> Vl.mark(:line)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "evaluations", type: :quantitative, title: "Search fitness evaluations")
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "best", type: :quantitative, title: "Best archived fitness")
  |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "setting", type: :nominal)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:detail, "series", type: :nominal)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:tooltip, "series", type: :nominal)

Kino.VegaLite.new(fig_search)
```

### Observed reference run

On the tested CPU runtime, all 36 runs completed and all 12 matched settings
agreed across implementations in final fitness, counters, and every recorded
generation-trace field. No run reached fitness 10. All six mixed-family
seed/patience settings classified the four signs correctly; repeating them through
three implementations gives 18 correct-classification runs, not 18 independent
search successes.

Fitness ranged from approximately 0.5613 to 5.5033, with 68-123 search evaluations.
These measurements show agreement under this protocol and budget. They do not
estimate a general convergence rate or establish a speed ranking. The live tables
above recompute the outcomes; other backends or near ties may produce different
long trajectories.

The figure shows actor traces to avoid drawing the three coincident copies over
one another. The agreement table checks the final counters and scores. The
authoring validation additionally compared every saved trace field.

## 9.4 Summary

Testing neuroevolution requires evidence at several levels. Mutation contracts
protect the genotype. Actor tests protect message and reset semantics. Numerical
comparisons protect the translation. Protocol tests ask whether fitness represents
the intended task. Repeated search runs measure behavior under a declared budget.

| Question | Evidence in this notebook | Limit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Did an operator do its job? | Independent postconditions and recorded mutation walks | Finite fixtures do not cover all graphs |
| Do the implementations agree? | Shared draws, exact structural comparison, tolerant numerical comparison | A long trajectory can branch at a near tie |
| Are identities meaningful? | Clone-reference, history, template, and fingerprint regressions | The fingerprint is deliberately coarse |
| Does the network compute XOR? | Fresh-state, all-order, ablation, and analytical-control tests | XOR remains a very small task |
| Did this search converge? | Raw runs, declared goal, budgets, archived-genotype replay | Three seeds per setting are insufficient for broad claims |

### Validation record

The default notebook executes **189 named assertion rows**:
51 inherited core/neural/operator checks, 83 contract regressions, 21 diagnostic
protocol checks, and 34 architecture/numerical/checkpoint/tick checks.
All passed on Elixir 1.18.4 / OTP 27 / Nx 1.0.0. The 36 search runs also passed
archive replay and evaluation-budget checks.

Kino table and VegaLite construction were executed, and the CSV download event
was tested with `Kino.Test`. Validation used checksum-verified package sources
at the pinned versions through local paths because registry access was unreliable.
The notebook retains ordinary `Mix.install` declarations. Browser-rendered
Livebook layout, other runtime versions, and GPU backends were not tested.

### Source audit and deliberate choices

| Source issue | Remaster treatment |
| --- | --- |
| Empty activation alternatives fall back to tanh | Reject without a history update |
| Cloning leaves ancestor IDs in history | Stable slot history; qualified live IDs change |
| Interface generation changes template equality | Stable template tags; instance ages are excluded |
| Fingerprints contain instance references | Layer/interface/operator summaries; collision is demonstrated |
| Recurrent XOR candidate looks suspicious | Test all orders, fresh resets, and input ablation |
| Feedforward filter listing has inconsistent arity | One explicit eligibility rule in both implementations |
| Printed add-neuron pools are restrictive | All legal sources and destinations are eligible |
| Historical retests are individual runs | Label them as history; provide separate matched raw runs |
| Unbounded source mutation retries | Bounded attempts and whole-RIM rollback, retained from Chapter 8 |

### Exercises

1. Add a test that swaps two hidden slots and updates every affected field.
   Decide which observations and fingerprints should remain unchanged.
2. Increase the search budget before increasing the number of seeds. Record both
   successes and failures; then estimate uncertainty across independent seeds.
3. Add a pruning operator with an explicit policy for history and reused slots.
   Write a failing connectivity test before changing the implementation.
4. Replace XOR with a task that requires memory. Define the reset rule first,
   then decide which recurrent-state tests should continue to pass.

